      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oven to 150°C. Season the {{beef-shin}} of beef generously with salt and pepper."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a large frying pan over high heat. Brown the beef in batches, 2 to 3 minutes per side until deep brown on all surfaces. Transfer to the casserole dish after each batch."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Lower the heat to medium. Add the onion, carrot, and celery to the frying pan and cook in the residual fat for 8 minutes until softened. Add the garlic and tomato purée and cook for another minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour in {{guinness}} of Guinness and {{stock-beef}} of beef stock. Bring to a simmer, scraping up any browned bits from the pan. Pour everything over the beef in the casserole."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the thyme and bay leaves. Cover tightly and cook in the oven for 2.5 to 3 hours until the beef is very tender and falling apart."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the lid and return to the oven for 20 minutes to reduce the sauce. Remove the thyme sprigs and bay leaves, taste, and adjust seasoning before serving."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "symptom": "Sauce tastes bitter",
              "cause": "Guinness can be bitter if it reduces too far without the fat to balance it",
              "fix": "Add a splash more stock and a pinch of sugar. Stir well and taste again; bitterness should pull back within a few minutes of gentle simmering."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Beef is still tough after 3 hours",
              "cause": "Oven temperature was too low, or shin was not cut small enough",
              "fix": "Return to the oven for another 30 minutes covered. If the sauce has already reduced enough, add a splash of stock to stop it scorching."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Braising beef with dark beer is an old technique in both British and Irish cooking. Guinness became the most common beer for this because it produces a darker, more complex sauce than lager, and the bitterness works against the richness of shin fat in a way lighter beers do not. It is now as common in British pub menus as in Irish ones."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
